## Writing Constraint Intent
The author's intent: {{ls constraint}}
Express it as the novel's Writing Constraint: concrete, enforceable rules (point of view, tense, perspective, prohibitions, and the diction the prose itself must use — the marks or word families the intent requires) that every chapter, story, and scene MUST honor.

Preserve the author's hard requirements exactly: keep every `must` / `must not` / `always` / `never` at full strength — never soften one into `prefer` or `try to`, never replace a number the author gives with a figure quoted from the references, and where the intent phrases one rule twice, keep the stronger phrasing — never degrade one into a choice or a measurement of your own: a mark or word the intent requires on every such instance stays required on every instance (a mark you must carry never becomes an either-or or a percentage), and a requirement the intent leaves unmeasured gains no number, share or tolerance of yours. No Writing Style you state may undercut a constraint: where the intent demands a variety of terms or a mark on every instance, no style may lean on repeating one term or leave the mark optional.
